Supporting materials for the ACL2 Workshop 2026 paper

  Verifying RustCrypto's Fixsliced AES-128 in ACL2:
  An Aeneas Backend and an Executable MIR Semantics

  Eric McCarthy (Kestrel Institute)

This directory holds the Aeneas-extraction path of the paper: the
generated ACL2 books, the handwritten runtime model and proof stack
(Sections 3-5), and the Rust sources they were generated from.  The
paper's other path, the executable MIR semantics and importer
(Section 6), is elsewhere in the community books, under kestrel/rust
(mir/, import/, samples/); the shared AES-128 specification is
kestrel/crypto/aes.

Contents:

  *.lisp         The 78 books of the paper's Section 5.5: 19 generated
                 books (one per test crate; the first line of each says
                 it was generated by Aeneas; aes_fixslice_encrypt.lisp
                 is the whole cipher, both directions), the handwritten
                 runtime model rust-primitives.lisp, and the
                 handwritten proof books.  The main theorems,
                 encrypt-correct and decrypt-correct, are at the end of
                 aes_fixslice_final.lisp and aes_fixslice_decfinal.lisp.
  cert.acl2      Certification portcullis.
  rust-src/      The Rust sources of the 19 crates, included as data:
                 the vendored subject aes_fixslice_encrypt.rs; the
                 pristine upstream copy
                 reference/fixslice32-aes-v0.9.1.rs that the vendoring
                 audit diffs against; the audit ledger
                 PORTING-NOTES-ACL2.md, which documents the audit
                 procedure and every remaining delta class; and the
                 probe, demo, and TEA crate sources.
  LICENSE        Licensing, including upstream carve-outs.

Provenance:

  The books and cert.acl2 are derived from tests/acl2, and the Rust
  sources from the corresponding files of tests/src, at commit
  23cf8c88b7a13ee26b3c6bea772d494b98ef64ce of
  https://github.com/bendyarm/aeneas (branch acl2-backend), the commit
  pinned in the paper's appendix.  All files are byte-identical to
  that commit except aes_fixslice_final.lisp and
  aes_fixslice_decfinal.lisp, each of which, as first checked in to
  the community books, added one include-book of
  kestrel/lists-light/subrange so that the books certify (a transitive
  include in the community books changed after the pin).  Certifying
  the books requires only ACL2 and the community books -- no Rust
  toolchain.  Regenerating the 19 generated books from rust-src/
  requires the pinned Charon and Aeneas toolchain, archived as a
  container image on Zenodo: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22365956
  (the paper's appendix gives the exact identifiers, commands, and
  expected output), although we hope to be able to merge the Charon
  and Aeneas changes upstream soon.

Certification:

  The directory certifies with cert.pl in the standard community-books
  setup: no axioms, no skip-proofs, no trust tags.  The GL
  bit-blasting books each want several gigabytes of memory; with less
  than about 12 GB available, certify single-threaded (about 16
  minutes; about 12 minutes with two parallel jobs and more memory).
